statement:
Let $p$ be a prime, let $a\geq1$, put $q=p^a$, let $k_0=\mathbf F_q$ be a field with $q$ elements, and let $k$ be an algebraic closure of $k_0$ with structure homomorphism $\iota:k_0\to k$. For every integer $n\geq1$:

1. the set $k^{(n)}=\{x\in k:x^{q^n}=x\}$ of roots of $X^{q^n}-X$ in $k$ is a subfield of $k$ with exactly $q^n$ elements;
2. $k^{(n)}$ is the only subfield of $k$ with $q^n$ elements;
3. $k^{(n)}\supseteq\iota(k_0)$, and $\iota(k_0)=k^{(1)}$ is the unique subfield of $k$ with $q$ elements.

Consequently $\mathbf F_{q^n}:=k^{(n)}$, 'the unique subfield of $k$ with $q^n$ elements', is well defined, and it is a $k_0$-algebra through $\iota$.
